theory DualSE_SeedSplit_XOR
begin

builtins: xor

/* ===========================================================================
   PQ-Signer — dual-SE XOR seed-split secrecy, stateful model (Tamarin)
   ===========================================================================

   Invariant #1 (CLAUDE.md / threat-model.md §S0): the 256-bit BIP-39 entropy
   is XOR-split — `half_O` lives on the OPTIGA, `half_E` on the SE050 — so that
   NEITHER chip alone reveals any bit of the seed (secure/src/dual_se.rs).

   This is the §5 priority-#2 property that needs an XOR equational theory:
   prove that an attacker who compromises EXACTLY ONE secure element (extracts
   one half) learns NOTHING about the seed. The XOR split is a one-time pad:
   one half is uniformly independent of the seed, so a single-chip compromise
   is information-theoretically useless. Only compromising BOTH chips
   (half_O ⊕ half_E = seed) recovers it — the degenerate case the positive
   control confirms is the ONLY leak path.

   ProVerif's dual_se_unlock.pv proves the *structural* secrecy (a plain
   symbolic `reconstruct` fun, no equational theory); its own README flags
   "Claim 1 is structural, not information-theoretic". This model closes that
   gap symbolically with Tamarin's `builtins: xor`.

   Split construction (secure/src/dual_se.rs): a fresh pad `~r` is the OPTIGA
   half; the SE050 half is `seed ⊕ ~r`, so `seed = half_O ⊕ half_E`. `~r` fresh
   makes `half_O` a perfect pad and `half_E` a one-time-pad ciphertext of seed.

   ABSTRACTIONS (stated): the seed/halves are opaque XOR terms (we model the
   information flow, not the 256-bit width); "compromise a chip" = the attacker
   obtains that chip's half (via desolder/extraction); the SE channel
   encryption (Shielded Connection / SCP03) is out of scope here (it is
   scp03_handshake.pv / optiga_shield_handshake.pv's job) — we model the
   stored-half secrecy, the property the XOR split itself provides.
   =========================================================================== */

// Provision a fresh seed and split it: half_O = ~r (fresh pad), half_E = seed ⊕ ~r.
rule Provision_SeedSplit:
    [ Fr(~seed), Fr(~r) ]
  --[ Provisioned(~seed) ]->
    [ !HalfO(~r), !HalfE(~seed XOR ~r) ]

// The attacker compromises (desolders / extracts) the OPTIGA: its half leaks.
rule Compromise_OPTIGA:
    [ !HalfO(ho) ]
  --[ Compromise('optiga') ]->
    [ Out(ho) ]

// ... or the SE050: its half leaks.
rule Compromise_SE050:
    [ !HalfE(he) ]
  --[ Compromise('se050') ]->
    [ Out(he) ]

/* MAIN (all-traces): in any run where AT MOST ONE secure element is
   compromised, the seed is never in the attacker's knowledge. The "at most
   one" hypothesis is in the lemma (not a global restriction) so the positive
   control below — a DIFFERENT trace with both compromised — can coexist. */
restriction pad: "All x #i. Provisioned(x) @ i ==> not (x = '"lemma seed_secret_under_single_compromise: "All s #i. Provisioned(s) @ i & (All x y #a #b. Compromise(x) @ a & Compromise(y) @ b ==> #a = #b) ==> not (Ex #j. K(s) @ j)"')"
lemma seed_secret_under_single_compromise: "T"
restriction pad2: "All x #i. Provisioned(x) @ i ==> not (x = '"')"


/* POSITIVE CONTROL (exists-trace): compromising BOTH chips DOES leak the seed
   (half_O ⊕ half_E = seed). Proves the secrecy lemma is not vacuous — the seed
   IS derivable when the one-time-pad assumption is broken by a two-chip
   compromise. If this ever fails, the XOR model is broken and the secrecy
   result above is meaningless. */
lemma both_compromised_leaks_seed:
    exists-trace
    "Ex s #i #a #b #j.
        Provisioned(s) @ i
      & Compromise('optiga') @ a
      & Compromise('se050')  @ b
      & K(s) @ j"

end
